# Server-First Architecture — RSC & Server Actions

## Overview
This project follows a **Server-First** approach based on the Next.js App Router model.

> Server Components are the default.  
> Client Components are the exception.

The architecture prioritizes:
- React Server Components (RSC)
- Server Actions for mutations
- Explicit caching and revalidation
- Minimal client-side JavaScript

---

## 1. Default to Server Components

- All components are **Server Components by default**
- Add `"use client"` only when absolutely required:
  - browser-only APIs (window, document)
  - local interactive state (onChange, animations)
  - client-only libraries

Rules:
- Never mark a route root as `"use client"` unless unavoidable
- Keep client components small and isolated (client islands)

---

## 2. Data Fetching Rules

- Fetch core page data on the server:
  - inside `page.tsx`
  - inside server-only utilities under `api/`
- Do not fetch primary data in `useEffect`
- Client-side fetching is allowed only for:
  - purely interactive UX
  - non-critical, secondary data

Use:
- Streaming and Suspense where appropriate
- `loading.tsx` for route-level loading states

---

## 3. Mutations: Server Actions First

- All create/update/delete flows must use **Server Actions** by default
- Avoid REST endpoints unless required for:
  - third-party webhooks
  - external clients
  - admin/system integrations

A Server Action must own:
- input validation
- database transaction
- cache invalidation
- returning minimal data for UI updates

---

## 4. Cache as a First-Class Concern

- Caching is part of domain design
- Always define cache boundaries intentionally
- After a mutation:
  - explicitly invalidate affected caches
  - ensure UI consistency without client refetch hacks

---

## 5. Server / Client Boundaries

- Server-only code:
  - database access
  - admin operations
  - secrets
- Must never be imported by client components

Recommended patterns:
- `*.server.ts` for server-only modules
- `*.client.ts` for client-only wrappers if needed

---

## 6. Supabase Usage (Server-First)

- Prefer server-side Supabase access using SSR clients
- Never expose service-role keys to the client
- Client components must not perform privileged mutations

---

## 7. Validation & Safety

- Validate all external input at runtime (forms, params, actions)
- Fail fast with user-friendly errors
- Do not rely on type safety alone

---

## Core Principle

> “If it can be server-rendered, it should be server-rendered.  
> Client-side code is an exception, not the default.”